<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/8th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Umayyad conquest of Sind</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/711">711 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Umayyad conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a> was the campaign of 711 CE in which forces led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-qasim">Muhammad ibn Qasim</a> conquered Sind, in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> valley in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. It brought the region under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> and marked the eastern limit of early Arab expansion into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>. Accounts of the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> treat it as part of the empire's simultaneous expansion at both ends of its realm.</p></div>
